{"source_id": "W2082988750", "title": "Test-enhanced learning: taking memory tests improves long-term retention", "year": 2006, "authors": ["Henry L. Roediger", "Jeffrey D. Karpicke"], "abstract": "Taking a memory test not only assesses what one knows, but also enhances later retention, a phenomenon known as the testing effect. In two experiments, students studied prose passages and either restudied or took a test. In Experiment 1, students who took a test after studying a passage recalled more of the passage a week later than did students who restudied it, even though the restudy group performed better on an immediate test. In Experiment 2, the benefit of testing was greater when the test was repeated, and testing produced better long-term retention than restudying regardless of whether feedback was provided."}
